course: dictionary
lesson: 8861
title: Shop: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Shop can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action). A shop is a place where you buy things, like a toy shop or a corner shop. To shop is to go and buy things. «A loaf has a set number of slices, and a shop sells only whole loaves.» Here shop is the place.
explain: The words around shop tell you which meaning you need. «I'm off to the shops» means the local shopping street. «He shopped me to the head» is different: in UK slang, to shop someone means to tell on them. Always ask: does this make sense as a place, an action, or telling on someone?
explain: A workshop is a place where things are made or fixed, so it is a kind of shop-like place with a different job. «Talk shop» means talk about work, and «shut up shop» means close or stop. The same word can change its meaning when the words next to it change.
explain: Some words belong to certain places. Americans often say «store» where we say shop. In Australia, a «servo» is a petrol station, and in the UK, «the offie» is an off-licence. When you are not sure, check the context first, then a dictionary.
fact: Shop is a noun (a place to buy things) and a verb (to go and buy things).
fact: «Shopping» means buying things, or the things you bought.
fact: A workshop is a place where things are made or fixed.
fact: To shop someone, in UK slang, means to tell the police or a teacher about them.
fact: In formal writing, a shop may be called a retail establishment or a vendor.
exercise: «A loaf has a set number of slices, and a shop sells only whole loaves.» In this sentence, what is a shop? | A kind of loaf | To make a loaf | A trip on a bus | *A place where you buy things
exercise: «He shopped me to the head.» What does shopped mean here? | Walked me to the head office | *Told on me to the head teacher | Made me a cup of tea | Bought me a present
exercise: What is a workshop? | A shop that sells only shoes | *A place where things are made or fixed | A shop that is closed for the day | A place to sleep
test: What does «I'm off to the shops» mean? | Going to the seaside | Going to bed early | *Going to the local shopping street | Going to close the school
test: What does «talk shop» mean? | Sell things at the door | *Talk about work | Buy things loudly | Shout at the till
exam: What does «retail establishment» mean in a formal text? | A library | *A shop | A park | A bus stop
